# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A query is exactly one read**, and it answers with a shape or an array of them —
# detached from the database, so nothing downstream can lazily load or write through what
# it was handed.
#
# There is no Result here. Finding nothing is an **answer**, not a failure: a query that may
# find nothing answers with an array, possibly empty, and a query that asserts it found one
# answers with the shape or raises. An envelope would make every caller unwrap a value that
# was never in doubt.
#
# **A query never calls another query.** That is two reads wearing one name, the second
# invisible to whoever asked, and it is the shape an N+1 arrives in. Where a caller needs
# two reads it asks for two.
class Query
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # A query has no Result to carry a refusal, and wrapping one would make every caller
  # unwrap a value that was never in doubt. So it raises, like every other query failure.
  #
  # An operation implementing `anonymous_call` instead of `call` runs before anyone is
  # identified and is not checked. That is a property of the class, never of the caller.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    raise Permission::Refused, permission unless permits?(actor)

    perform(actor, arguments)
  end

  # **Setup asks what state is legal, not who may reach it.** A test building a booking to
  # cancel is not testing who may build one, and assembling a grant bag to get there teaches
  # nothing. `test_call` skips the permission check and nothing else — a query writes no audit
  # entry to skip. The typed arguments and the shapes assertion still run, so what it answers is
  # what the application would answer, which is the whole point of `no-test-factories`.
  #
  # **A test uses it for the subject too, not only for setup.** A permission is the class name,
  # so authorisation is class-sized — an actor holds the permission or does not — and the check
  # is this base class's, identical for every operation and proven once in its own suite. An
  # operation has no authorisation behaviour of its own for a test to assert.
  #
  # **It raises outside the test environment**, so it cannot become a production door. That is
  # the one thing an unchecked entry point must promise, and it is why this is a method rather
  # than an actor that says yes to everything: such an actor is a value, and a value can be
  # constructed anywhere, including in production.
  def self.test_call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless defined?(::Rails) && ::Rails.env.test?
      raise "#{name}.test_call is for tests: there is no unchecked door outside the test environment"
    end

    perform(actor, arguments)
  end

  # The half of `call` that runs once the door has been opened. Both entry points share it,
  # so the only difference between them is the check itself.
  def self.perform(actor, arguments)
    operation = new(**arguments)
    answer = operation.__perform__(actor)
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    answer = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with shapes, got #{answer.class}" unless shapes?(answer)

    answer
  end
<%- if auth -%>
  private_class_method :perform
<%- end -%>

  def self.shapes?(answer)
    return answer.all? { |entry| entry.is_a?(Shape) } if answer.is_a?(Array)

    answer.is_a?(Shape)
  end
  private_class_method :shapes?

<%- if auth -%>

  private

  # Whoever the door was opened for. Most operations never read it, which is why it is here
  # rather than in every initializer.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  # **Dispatches with an implicit receiver**, so it works whether the operation left its
  # entry point public or made it private. `send` would work too and is banned: it steps
  # over `private`, routes around the call graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A
  # base class that writes it cannot ask an application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on — and `Shipshape/OnlyTheDoorIsCalled` refuses the attempt at
  # the call site, where no visibility keyword can be stepped over.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** The check
    # above needed it; most operations do not, and forcing `actor:` into every initializer
    # would put a keyword in fifty constructors so that three could read it. Set here, and
    # available to any operation that wants it through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
